<div class="pk-rest-warning">
	<div class="pk-victory-scanlines"></div>
	<div class="pk-rest-warning-inner">

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-3 mb-3">
			<div class="pk-glados-portrait" aria-hidden="true">🤖</div>
			<div>
				<div class="pk-glados-name">GLaDOS</div>
				<div class="pk-glados-title">WARNING: Aperture Science Rest Protocol</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="pk-glados-speech" data-bind="typewriter: true">
			<p>
				I see you've found the Rest function.
				How... <em>pragmatic.</em>
				Most test subjects discover it the moment things become
				even slightly difficult.
			</p>
			<p>
				I should inform you -- and I want to be very clear
				about this -- that using this feature will
				<strong>permanently disqualify you from a Hard Mode victory.</strong>
				This flag is stored in your test record and
				<em>cannot be reversed.</em>
				Not by me. Not by you. Not by anyone.
			</p>
			<p>
				You may, of course, proceed.
				Plenty of subjects have.
				We called them <em>quitters,</em> but only in the internal documentation.
			</p>
			<p>
				Or you could close this prompt and
				push through on your own.
				I find that <em>deeply</em> unlikely, but science demands I
				present all possible outcomes.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="d-flex justify-content-end gap-2 mt-3">
			<button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm"
					data-bind="click: cancelRest">
				Cancel
			</button>
			<button type="button" class="btn btn-danger btn-sm"
					data-bind="click: confirmRest">
				Proceed with Rest
			</button>
		</div>

	</div>
</div>
